I'm Callie reading for reviewer Heidi  Kemp's you're about to learn the hard  way that effort alone isn't enough to  reach my level Dragon Ball Z Kakarot  begins right where the anime does  introducing us to Goku and his son Gohan  just before the Saiyans are set to  invade earth revealing Goku's true say  in heritage and setting off a chain of  events that threaten both earth and the  entire universe but the story we've seen  played out in many Dragon Ball Z games  over the years but unlike recent  examples Packer OTT tells it the tale by  way of a narrative driven RPG rather  than a strictly combat focused game it  gives life to the world and story of DBZ  in a refreshing way giving us a glimpse  into what life is like for Goku and his  many companions outside of battles for  the fate of the universe lies in the  balance all of Dragon Ball Z's major  story arcs are contained here but among  all of these massive earth-shattering  sagas and intense fights are numerous  smaller stories and character  interactions that many games have simply  glossed over the game structure is split  into parts free-roaming exploration  sequences with a semi open-world battle  scenes against foes big and small and  cutscenes where you watch some of the  most dramatic story moments of DBZ play  out in gorgeous and engine renditions  there's a good balance between all of  these it really feels like you're  spending too long watching a cutscene or  that you're thrust into constant battle  without being able to take a moment to  catch your breath  sometimes the exploration sequences can  seem a little over long but a lot of  that depends on how much time you want  to spend doing side quests and hunting  collectibles it's not essential to spend  a lot of time on side pursuits but it  does reap benefits and while you're  flying around the big vibrant  environments it's easy to be swept up in  exploring the DBZ world itself one  striking thing about DBZ Kakarot is how  it showcases the large cast of the anime  you begin the game as Goku but as things  progress in the story you assume control  over several other characters like Gohan  piccolo Vegeta trunks and others  familiar faces like Krillin Yamcha and  Android a teenager and combat as  assistant many of the other supporting  DBZ cast members make appearances in  SciQuest and story scenes as well Kraven  gone it's  - Dragon Ball Z is a series where  character relationships and interactions  are important and that really comes  through in a lot of the non-combat story  bits you see piccolo warm up to young  Gohan chi Chi's tough mother role the  fighters bonding outside of battle and  much more even relatively minor  characters like launch and who are have  side quests that showcase funny  interactions silly scenarios and even  genuinely sad and touching moments  seeing so many DBZ characters given  their moment to shine is great and it  helps you forget that a lot of the side  quests are fairly typical RPG kill these  enemies or collect these item Affairs of  course it would it be Dragonball Z  without combat while the 3d action  driven combat takes some getting used to  at first once you've got a decent handle  on the controls they'll be flying around  shooting off key blasts and Kamehameha  is like a pro you control a single  character with an arsenal of attacks  basic short and long range attacks  powerful special skills like energy  blasts a boost to get up close to the  opponent defensive techniques to guard  dodge and counter an attacking foe and  even power up into stronger forms it's a  careful balance since many of these  abilities cost ki which requires  charging mid battle but leaves you  vulnerable when doing so when your  tension gauge is full you can send your  warrior into a super-powered state where  you can change special texts into each  other causing some serious devastation  and if you need a little help you can  call on a CPU companion to do their own  attack it's an intriguing combat system  and the 3d aerial movement element is  unique but there's less depth to it than  it seems on the surface most normal  enemies and even a few bosses can be  patterned to make fighting them much  easier on top of that enemy variety  outside of main story battles tends to  be lacking particularly the annoying  cannon fodder foes that will interrupt  you during times when you just want to  explore the fighting still has some  standout moments particularly during big  boss fights with enemies without massive  incredibly damaging energy attacks that  force a rapid change in strategy and  sometimes even change the entire  perspective of combat overcoming some of  the nastiest things Dragon Ball Z's like  chronic villains toss at you with  skillful dodging and well-timed attacks  is immensely satisfying and somewhat  makes up for all the combat time wasted  punching the same robots over and over  again Dragon Ball Z karotz modern  semi-open approach to telling the saga  of DBZ despite some minor issues is a  good one  zooming around the environments and  seeing the world up close as a blast and  it's great being able to interact with  so many fun DBZ characters and see  stories that usually get passed over for  game adaptations and even though combat  can be a bit lacking when the big  battles happen they feel suitably epic  and engaging if you're looking for an  enjoyable way to see the life and times  of adult Goku through a new perspective  Dragon Ball Z Kakarot will grant your  wish  [Music]  you
 

